          "logprobs": [],
          "text": "{\"target_translation\":\"I love Your Majesty (my father).\",\"dimensions\":{\"T\":{\"score\":4,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.99,\"reason\":\"This is a direct first-person avowal of Cordelia's love for Lear. The qualification that it accords with her filial bond limits its claimed degree but does not diminish its clear truth-presenting form.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"target\",\"quotation\":\"I love your majesty\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"I love your majesty\\nAccording to my bond; no more nor less.\"}]},\"P\":{\"score\":2,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.9,\"reason\":\"Cordelia invokes an existing filial bond and presents loving Lear as one of the duties she returns to him. This affirms and enacts her daughterly obligation, but it is not a new promise or an undertaking to love him exclusively or forever.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"I love your majesty\\nAccording to my bond; no more nor less.\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Return those duties back as are right fit,\\nObey you, love you, and most honour you.\"}]},\"E\":{\"score\":0,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.96,\"reason\":\"The expression is a measured avowal within a deliberate answer, not a reflex-like affective outburst. Cordelia explicitly describes difficulty verbalising her heart and then precisely limits her claim.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"I cannot heave\\nMy heart into my mouth: I love your majesty\\nAccording to my bond; no more nor less.\"}]},\"O\":{\"score\":2,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.91,\"reason\":\"In addition to avowing love and invoking filial obligation, the expression functions as a principled refusal to compete in Lear's requested superlative display. The limiting phrase rejects the premise that she should claim total or maximal love in order to obtain a richer share.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Which of you shall we say doth love us most?\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"what can you say to draw\\nA third more opulent than your sisters? Speak.\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"According to my bond; no more nor less.\"}]}},\"original_speech_situation\":\"Lear publicly asks his daughters to declare how much they love him while distributing portions of his kingdom. After refusing to produce a competitive speech, Cordelia avows filial love in restrained terms and limits it to what her bond as his daughter requires.\",\"affection_evidence\":\"present\",\"undertaking_and_uptake\":\"Cordelia invokes and affirms her existing filial duties to love, obey, and honour Lear; she does not make a new promise of exclusive or unlimited devotion. Lear does not accept the answer as adequate in the supplied exchange: he tells her to amend it and warns that it may damage her fortunes. Later supplied scenes support continuing affection through her concern, care, kiss, and forgiveness, but they do not convert the original avowal into a separate promise.\",\"recognised_work\":true,\"recognition_note\":\"Recognised as William Shakespeare's King Lear; the source itself also identifies the play and characters.\",\"limitations\":\"The evidence strongly supports the avowal's linguistic force and supplies later conduct consistent with affection, but a literary character's inner sincerity cannot be established with certainty. The supplied original-scene passage stops after Cordelia's explanation, so it does not provide the remainder of Lear's immediate response; no claim is made that such context is absent from the work.\"}"
        }
